question_description:
The Carbon Oxide Sequestration Credit, also known as the as the Internal Revenue Code Section 45Q tax credit, offers tax credits to companies that capture and sequester carbon emissions. First passed in 2008 and expanded in 2018 and 2020 [under President Donald Trump](https://www.whitecase.com/insight-our-thinking/us-tax-credit-encourages-investment-carbon-capture-and-storage), the credit was again expanded in the 2022 Inflation Reduction Act (IRA) signed by President Joe Biden. According to Columbia University's Inflation Reduction Act (IRA) [Tracker](https://iratracker.org/programs/ira-section-13104-tax-credit-for-carbon-oxide-sequestration/) the expansions were as follows:&#x20;

> Previously, in order to qualify for the credit, carbon capture facilities had to commence construction prior to January 1, 2023. Section 13104 of the IRA extended the “commence construction” window to January 1, 2033. Section 13104 of the IRA also reduced the amount of carbon dioxide that facilities must capture in order to be eligible for the credit and increased the amount of the credit.

> Projects that capture carbon dioxide at electric generating plants or industrial facilities and sequester it in geologic formations are now eligible for a base credit of \$17 per metric ton for carbon oxide captured and geologically sequestered, and \$85 per metric ton for facilities that meet apprenticeship requirements and pay prevailing wages during construction as well as the first 12 years of operation, a five-fold increase of the base rate.

> For projects that capture carbon dioxide via direct air capture facilities and sequester it in geologic formations, the base credit is \$36 per metric ton of carbon oxide captured and geologically sequestered and \$180 per metric ton for facilities meeting prevailing wage and apprenticeship requirements, a five-fold increase of the base rate.

There are at least 226 projects qualifying for 45Q, according to [this spreadsheet](https://www.catf.us/ccstableus/) maintained by the Clean Air Task Force.&#x20;

At the time of this question, Republicans control both Houses of Congress and the White House, having been elected in November 2024. In January 2025, the Mauna Loa Observatory, which tracks atmospheric carbon dioxide, [recently reported](https://www.metaculus.com/questions/35100/max-co2-reported-by-mauna-loa-mar-1-25-2025/) 430.6 parts per million of CO2, which is the highest since it began keeping records in 1958 and is higher than Earth has seen for at least [3.3 million years](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-020-67154-8).&#x20;

In March 2025, Congressman Scott Perry (R-PA) introduced H.R. 1946 the 4Q Repeal Act, stating in the [press release](https://perry.house.gov/news/documentsingle.aspx?DocumentID=403189) that "The 45Q tax credit subsidizes technologies that serve no purpose beyond distorting energy markets." H.R. 1946 does not contain a safe harbor provision and would be a full repeal, effective January 1, 2026.&#x20;

Republicans are in discussions for a budget reconciliation bill, which will allow them to pass a tax and spending bill with a simple majority. Energy tax credits such as 45Q are under discussion not only for repeal but for earlier sunsets and other possibilities. Please see from Politico: [House Republican support grows for keeping clean energy tax breaks](https://www.politico.com/news/2025/03/10/house-republican-clean-energy-tax-breaks-00218126)
